              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.15"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.11,
            0.882,
            0.17
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "BCRP is a parallelogram, so CR = BP. BP = PA and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle AQP = \\angle CQR"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (vertically opposite angles)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.173,
            0.88,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Combining with CR ‖BA, ∆APQ ≅ ∆ CRQ. (Why?)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.477,
            0.217,
            0.751,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This gives two equalities."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.242,
            0.881,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "First, AQ = QC, so Q is the midpoint of AC."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.476,
            0.283,
            0.837,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Second, PQ = QR, so "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "PQ = \\frac{PR}{2} = \\frac{BC}{2}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.327,
            0.882,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have deduced the following important fact. It can be considered a converse of the Midpoint Theorem, for reasons we will see in Exercise 22 at the end of the chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.39,
            0.883,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 7 (Converse of the Midpoint Theorem):",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The line drawn through the midpoint of one side of a triangle and parallel to another side bisects the third side (and the resulting segment has half the length of the parallel side)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.466,
            0.882,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us see a different reasoning to justify Theorem 7. Read this short proof carefully."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.53,
            0.424,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.119,
                0.53,
                0.424,
                0.717
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.219,
                0.717,
                0.317,
                0.736
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.16"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.44,
            0.519,
            0.883,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Another proof of Theorem 7.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Recall our previous setup: the line parallel to BC through midpoint P of AB meets AC at point Q. Ignore this line for now and instead consider the midpoint M of AC. We want to show M = Q. We have PM ‖ BC by the Midpoint Theorem. Therefore line PM = line PQ, because there is a unique line parallel to BC through P. As lines PM and PQ coincide, they must intersect AC in the same point. Thus M = Q. This is what we wanted!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.76,
            0.884,
            0.897
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We used the Midpoint Theorem to prove its converse! This may look like cheating because, as emphasised earlier, the truth of a statement and of its converse are separate issues. But there is no mistake here. There is a key new point in this proof, namely that there is a unique line through a given point and parallel to a given line. This proof illustrates that sometimes we can use a theorem to prove its converse."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
